Jukka Lahtinen wrote:

[ valid point about platform independent compression snipped ]

> But are you sure compression would be needed? The generic format would
> only be needed for transferring the files from one platform to another, the
> files would not be always stored in that format.

I was under the impression that there would/should only be one save
file format. It is not necessary, but I think would make sense; why
implement two different versions for I/O if basically it's the same
requirement.

That has been the reason why I mentioned ASN.1/BER. You would have
- a unique specification
- generated data structures for platform and language (actually C)
- handling of optional data structures depending on the NH features
   compiled in (though making it possible to let a save file from one
   NH configuration run in another NH configuration with different
   features enabled)[*]
- standardized transfer syntax, which is compact enough to make
   compression not that necessary (compared to XML)

(As I noted earlier, there are also drawbacks...)

[*] That's just the vision; must think of that, whether it is possible
at all given NHs design principles.

> At least the compression wouldn't need to be integrated in the export /
> import utilities. The files could be first imported, then packed (for
> example with zip), transferred to the other machine, unpacked and imported
> to that platform. 

Yes, if you have the import/export sepatated from save/restore then
it is a possible approach. You would have tools to convert from NH
save format to some transfer format, I suppose? The advantage would
be that you don't have to rewrite the core I/O.

How would you think to handle save files from versions with different
features enabled? (The point I worried about in my thoughts; see [*].)
The easier way would be to assume a standard feature configuration.

